WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140
Welcome back to SpaceX Daily, Episode 38.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.000
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.920
Today is July 20th, 2026.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.180
From Starbase to orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.820
Starships July 23rd target now includes

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.680
the first V3 satellites, determining whether the vehicle

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.180
can deliver operational gigabit links before year end.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.360
SpaceX has set the next Starship Integrated Flight Test

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.820
attempt for July 23rd, carrying the initial batch

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.820
of Starlink V3 satellites.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.400
The flight will evaluate payload deployment performance

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.780
at the higher mass and power levels of the V3 design.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.280
This test follows the prior scrub and focuses

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.560
on closing the timeline to routine V3 service.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.460
Observers will watch for successful upper stage restart

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.700
and precise orbit insertion data.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.060
The V3 satellites represent the first operational hardware

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.480
intended to close the gap between current Starlink throughput

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.880
and the targeted gigabit class links.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.540
While Starship pushes the performance envelope,

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.340
Falcon 9 operations continue to surface edge case

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.840
reliability questions.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.060
A Falcon 9 on the Starlink 17-39 mission

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.040
aborted at ignition from SLC4E at Vandenberg.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.400
Booster B1082 reached its 23rd flight before the hold.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.920
The vehicle performed a safe shutdown

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.660
with no reported damage to pad or hardware.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.140
Teams are reviewing telemetry to identify the ignition

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:34.640
anomaly.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.180
This marks the second consecutive last second abort

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.360
on Falcon 9 vehicles operating from separate pads.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.780
The same ignition sequencing that triggered the abort

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.120
is examined in depth in today's engineering deep dive.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.380
One thing worth watching is how Falcon 9

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.260
last second abort at ignition remain rare

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.760
but have now occurred twice in quick succession

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.200
across separate pads.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.000
The Vandenberg event on B1082 adds data points

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.040
on engine start sequencing under flight ready loads.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.360
Resolution depends on root cause analysis

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.860
of propellant conditioning or valve timing

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.200
before the next scheduled Starlink mission.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.960
From an engineering standpoint, the last second ignition

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.440
aborts on Falcon 9 center on the precise sequencing

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.900
of propellant valves and igniter timing

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.360
under tank head pressure.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.340
The system must confirm stable flow of both RP1

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.640
and liquid oxygen into the Merlin combustion chambers

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.340
before spark initiation.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.660
Any pressure or temperature deviation outside narrow bands

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.800
triggers an automatic shutdown to protect the turbopumps.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.360
This protective logic trades a few extra seconds

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.920
of countdown margin for hardware survival,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.120
keeping the booster reusable

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.260
without post flight inspection of damaged components.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.920
The same monitoring architecture now operates

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.440
on vehicles that have already flown more than 20 times

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.220
where cumulative seal wear or minor thermal distortion

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.460
can shift the exact moment stable ignition occurs.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.360
Engineers therefore rely on real time sensor fusion

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.060
rather than fixed timers, allowing the vehicle

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.900
to adapt to each booster's individual history.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.860
When an abort happens at T zero, the data captured

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.420
during that final half second of valve motion

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.820
becomes the primary input for the next pad flow test.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.300
Over repeated flights, this feedback loop steadily

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.560
narrows the uncertainty band around ignition reliability.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.240
Each successful start after an earlier hold

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.860
demonstrates that the abort threshold is set conservatively

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.920
enough to catch anomalies, yet not so tightly

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.380
that normal variation halts every attempt.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:50.020
The result is higher cadence without increasing the risk

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.800
of an in-flight engine failure

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.120
that would end the booster's reuse career.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.320
The approach mirrors practices

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.020
in other high cycle turbo machinery fields

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.200
where sensor driven shutdowns preserve capital equipment

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.680
across hundreds of starts.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.620
In Falcon 9's case, the accumulated flight history

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.900
on B1082 itself supplies the statistical baseline

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.820
that lets teams distinguish one off sensor noise

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.480
from a developing valve or conditioning issue.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.560
The same data set also feeds forward

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.800
into Merlin engine block upgrades.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.660
Small adjustments to valve timing curves

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.200
or pre-chill protocols can be validated

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.900
on the next static fire or launch attempt,

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.280
incrementally raising the probability

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.720
that a given booster will reach its design reuse target

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.540
because each abort supplies high resolution telemetry

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.160
at essentially zero marginal cost beyond the delay itself.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.360
The program converts what looks like lost schedule

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.220
into a continuous reliability improvement loop.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.820
Over dozens of flights this loop

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.080
has already driven ignition success rates

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.800
above 99% on reused hardware.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.800
The current pair of holds simply supplies

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.320
the next calibration points.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.800
SpaceX will fly the Northrop Grumman MRV-1 mission

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.580
on a Falcon 9 from Florida.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.860
The spacecraft is designed for on-orbit satellite servicing

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:13.960
and repair tasks.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.860
Integration work is underway at the Cape

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.620
for a near-term launch window.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.160
This marks an expansion of commercial servicing contracts

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.060
beyond government payloads.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.700
The mission extends Falcon 9's role

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.040
into new classes of national security

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.340
and commercial satellite support flights.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.420
While Falcon 9 adds new mission classes,

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.340
Starlink continues signing sovereign customers.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.040
Iraq has signed a national agreement

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.260
allowing Starlink service rollout.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.200
The deal covers licensing and spectrum use

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.480
for residential and enterprise terminals.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.800
Deployment planning now shifts

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.860
to ground infrastructure and regulatory approvals.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.420
This adds another Middle East market

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.360
to the growing constellation footprint.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.640
Ground station siding and terminal certification

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.660
will determine how quickly service can reach users

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.060
after spectrum clearance.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.400
Starlink growth keeps fueling speculation

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.360
about affiliated compute needs.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.820
On the AI front, Elon Musk publicly rejected claims

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.900
of a $52 billion space X order

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.760
for approximately 1 million NVIDIA GB 300 GPUs

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.260
via Foxcon racks.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:27.020
The denial directly addresses speculation

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.680
linking Starlink growth to large-scale

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.940
terrestrial AI hardware purchases.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.060
No alternative compute procurement figures

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.800
were provided in the statement.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.120
The clarification leaves open how space X

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:43.240
or affiliated entities may still source accelerators

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.400
for future orbital or ground-based inference workloads.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.100
Finally, an expanded Falcon 9 upper stage

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.020
offers an unplanned lunar science opportunity.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.720
An expanded Falcon 9 upper stage is projected

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.340
to impact the lunar surface on August 5th.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.540
Trajectory models place the impact site

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:04.180
in a southern Highland region.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:07.540
This continues the pattern of controlled disposal

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.440
for spent stages after geosynchronous missions.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.660
Impact observations may provide data

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.240
on lunar regolith response.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.500
The event offers a low cost opportunity

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:20.780
to gather surface interaction measurements

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.840
without dedicated lunar probes.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.120
That closes today's engineering and market threads.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.360
Our space X lounge users noted the first confirmed engine swap

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:35.120
from booster 20 observed at McGregor on an outer raptor

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.520
with the unit having ignited during a prior attempt.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.100
Separate posts highlight the Falcon 9 ignition abort

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:48.580
on B108223 as the first such event on that booster since 2024,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.600
with commenters comparing telemetry timing

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.800
to earlier 2024 holds for pattern analysis.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.840
Additional threads discuss the upcoming Northrop Grumman

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.280
MRV-1 servicing mission and what it signals

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.280
for future commercial on-orbit repair contracts.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.640
Users also tracked the projected Falcon 9 upper stage lunar

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.900
impact on August 5th, noting its value

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.580
for passive regolith studies.

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.900
Starships next attempt keeps the engineering focus

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.700
on V3 satellite deployment performance.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.540
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

